  abstract = {Nine families in each of 3 categories—child abusive (CAB), distressed-nonabusive (DN), and nonproblem—were observed over 3 nonconsecutive days. The families were matched for sex and age of target child, number of siblings, SES, and 1- or 2-parent status; the mother was the abusing agent in all of the CAB families, who were offered a social-learning-based intervention designed to reduce coercive family interactions and increase parenting skills through accurate parental monitoring of child behaviors, consistent and contingent reinforcement of prosocial child behaviors, and consistent and contingent application of a nonphysical punishment technique. DN families featured children who repeatedly acted out physically aggressive behavior. Results support the assertion that abusive families function within a coercive family system. CAB mothers were significantly more aversive than nonproblem mothers in daily family interactions. Although they were not abusing agents, fathers in CAB families emitted aversive behavior at significantly higher rate than nonproblem fathers. Abused children did not differ from nonproblem children in overall rates of aversive behavior. CAB mothers also showed an inability to terminate aversive interchanges with children, punish aversive behavior effectively, or exhibit appropriate responses to prosocial behavior. The intervention was effective in interrupting the coercive interactional cycle that appears to exist between the abusive mother and the abused child. (15 ref) (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)},
