  pages = {272--284},
  publisher = {Routledge/Taylor \& Francis Group},
  location = {New York, NY, US},
  abstract = {The current chapter presents unipolar item response models (UIRMs). UIRMs have the potential for several extensions. The first extension lies in the choice of link functions. Here we considered only two, the log-logistic and the Weibull, but any non-negatively supported distribution function, such as the log-normal or log-Cauchy, could be used. Justification of the link function on substantive grounds would be essential. The UIRMs proposed here assumed that the probability of item endorsement was zero for those persons with a trait level at zero. Although this makes sense for measures of addictive disorders, it need not be the case for measures of other unipolar traits such as depression, chronic pain, or mobility assessment. The third extension would include an item parameter for the probability of an item's endorsement when the trait level is zero. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2025 APA, all rights reserved)},
