  abstract = {Describes 2 stimulus control processes by which some parent–child dyads occasionally escalate their aversive exchanges into progressively more coercive interactions. The compliance hypothesis suggests that aversive actions have instructional properties for the dyad and that parent compliance with such child instructions maintains behavior chains of increasing aversiveness. The predictability hypothesis suggests that social interactions are most likely to function as aversive stimuli in the dyad when delivered in unpredictable fashion by either party and that responses instrumental in reducing dyadic unpredictability maintain aversive behavior chains. Expectations derived from both hypotheses were evaluated in a series of correlational analyses of mother–child interactions obtained in extended baseline observations of 3 dyads (children's ages 4–12 yrs) seeking psychological help for severe interactional problems. More than 90\% of aversive exchanges were initiated by children rather than by their mothers, and high levels of child aversiveness were associated with low levels of maternal compliance. All 3 mothers were fairly consistent in their use of aversive attention during and following their children's episodes of aversive actions. Results provide tentative support for the predictability hypothesis. (27 ref) (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)},
  keywords = {Childrearing Practices,Mother Child Relations,Theory Verification},
